### ConfigProfile fields (no forced_login_method)

Source: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/config/src/profile_toml.rs

The `ConfigProfile` struct defines all fields valid inside `[profiles.<name>]` in config.toml. There is no `forced_login_method` field. Relevant local-Ollama fields include: `model`, `model_provider`, `oss_provider`, `approval_policy`, `sandbox_mode`, `model_reasoning_effort`, `tools`, `features`, and others listed — but NOT `forced_login_method`.

```rust
pub struct ConfigProfile {
    pub model: Option<String>,
    pub service_tier: Option<String>,
    pub model_provider: Option<String>,
    pub approval_policy: Option<AskForApproval>,
    pub approvals_reviewer: Option<ApprovalsReviewer>,
    pub sandbox_mode: Option<SandboxMode>,
    pub model_reasoning_effort: Option<ReasoningEffort>,
    pub plan_mode_reasoning_effort: Option<ReasoningEffort>,
    pub model_reasoning_summary: Option<ReasoningSummary>,
    pub model_verbosity: Option<Verbosity>,
    pub model_catalog_json: Option<AbsolutePathBuf>,
    pub personality: Option<Personality>,
    pub chatgpt_base_url: Option<String>,
    pub model_instructions_file: Option<AbsolutePathBuf>,
    pub experimental_compact_prompt_file: Option<AbsolutePathBuf>,
    pub include_permissions_instructions: Option<bool>,
    pub include_apps_instructions: Option<bool>,
    pub include_collaboration_mode_instructions: Option<bool>,
    pub include_environment_context: Option<bool>,
    pub experimental_use_unified_exec_tool: Option<bool>,
    pub tools: Option<ToolsToml>,
    pub web_search: Option<WebSearchMode>,
    pub analytics: Option<AnalyticsConfigToml>,
    pub tui: Option<ProfileTui>,
    pub windows: Option<WindowsToml>,
    pub features: Option<FeaturesToml>,
    pub oss_provider: Option<String>,
}
```

--------------------------------

### Inline config.toml example: custom provider with model_provider selection

Source: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/config/src/thread_config.rs

Complete TOML example showing how to select a custom provider via `model_provider` and define it under `[model_providers.<name>]` with a base URL, wire protocol, and feature flags. The default model is not set here but would be `model = "..."` at the top level.

```toml
model_provider = "local"

[model_providers.local]
name = "local"
base_url = "http://127.0.0.1:8061/api/codex"
wire_api = "responses"
requires_openai_auth = false
supports_websockets = true
supports_standalone_web_search = true

[features]
plugins = false
```

--------------------------------

### Deep merge of config layers via merge_toml_values

Source: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/config/src/merge.rs

Config layers are merged from lowest to highest precedence. Tables are recursively merged (overlay keys override base keys at every nesting level), while scalar values from the overlay completely replace the base value. This means `[apps._default]` in a higher-precedence layer (e.g., project or session) can override `[apps]` from a lower layer, and per-app `enabled` fields merge independently.

```rust
/// Merge config `overlay` into `base`, giving `overlay` precedence.
pub fn merge_toml_values(base: &mut TomlValue, overlay: &TomlValue) {
    merge_toml_values_at_path(base, overlay, &mut Vec::new());
}

fn merge_toml_values_at_path(base: &mut TomlValue, overlay: &TomlValue, path: &mut Vec<String>) {
    ...
    if let TomlValue::Table(overlay_table) = overlay
        && let TomlValue::Table(base_table) = base
    {
        ...
        for (key, value) in overlay_table {
            path.push(key.clone());
            if let Some(existing) = base_table.get_mut(&key) {
                merge_toml_values_at_path(existing, &value, path);
            } else {
                base_table.insert(key, normalized_with_key_aliases(&value, path));
            }
            path.pop();
        }
    } else {
        *base = normalized_with_key_aliases(overlay, path);
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Load effective configuration and metadata

Source: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/config/src/loader/README.md

Use load_config_layers_state to aggregate configuration layers and retrieve the effective config, origins, and layer list for UI display.

```rust
use codex_config::LoaderOverrides;
use codex_config::NoopThreadConfigLoader;
use codex_config::loader::load_config_layers_state;
use codex_exec_server::LOCAL_FS;
use codex_utils_absolute_path::AbsolutePathBuf;
use toml::Value as TomlValue;

let cli_overrides: Vec<(String, TomlValue)> = Vec::new();
let cwd = AbsolutePathBuf::current_dir()?;
let layers = load_config_layers_state(
    LOCAL_FS.as_ref(),
    &codex_home,
    Some(cwd),
    &cli_overrides,
    LoaderOverrides::default(),
    &NoopThreadConfigLoader,
).await?;

let effective = layers.effective_config();
let origins = layers.origins();
let layers_for_ui = layers.layers_high_to_low().collect::<Vec<_>>();
```

### codex-config loader > Layering model

Source: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/config/src/loader/README.md

Configuration layers follow a strict precedence model where top layers override bottom layers. The hierarchy ranges from MDM-delivered managed configurations and CLI session flags down to project-specific, user-profile, enterprise-managed, and system-level configuration files. Internally, the system stores these layers in order of increasing precedence to facilitate the merging process.

